<h1>Hinged Steel Belt Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A hinged steel belt conveyor uses interlocking steel plates connected by hinge pins to form a continuous, rugged conveying surface for metal chips, stampings, scrap, and heavy industrial debris. The overlapping plate design creates a solid belt that withstands hot parts, resists cutting oil and coolant, and handles sharp-edged scrap that would slice rubber or plastic belts. Hinged steel belt conveyors are the standard chip and scrap removal system in CNC machining centers, stamping presses, laser cutters, and metalworking shops worldwide.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Hinged Steel Belts Work</h2>
<p>Hinged steel belt construction begins with individual steel plates formed from sheet steel. Plate thickness and pitch (the hinge-to-hinge distance) increase with duty. Each plate has a formed hinge along one edge and a mating lip along the opposite edge. Hinge pins pass through the hinge barrels of adjacent plates, linking them into a continuous chain-like belt. The plates overlap when the belt runs on the flat carrying surface and separate slightly as the belt wraps around the head and tail sprockets.</p>
<p>The belt rides on side rails or wear strips inside a formed steel trough. Drive sprockets at the discharge end engage the hinge pins or side chains to pull the belt forward. A gravity or spring take-up at the tail end maintains belt tension. The belt's top (carrying) run transports material from the loading point to the discharge, where it wraps around the head sprocket and material falls by gravity into a bin, hopper, or secondary conveyor.</p>
<p>The return run travels below the carrying run inside the same trough. This enclosed design keeps chips, coolant, and debris contained. Some designs include a coolant collection trough beneath the return run that channels cutting fluid back to the machine's coolant tank for recirculation.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Hinged Belt Types</h2>
<h3>Standard Hinged Belt</h3>
<p>Standard hinged belts use flat overlapping plates with simple hinge-pin connections. They handle most chip types including turnings, borings, and small stampings. Light-duty belts use small pitches. Storch Magnetics lists a <a href="https://www.storchmagnetics.com/pages/steel-hinge-belt-conveyors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1.5 in. pitch hinge belt</a> for light duty and 2.5 in. pitch for light to medium duty. Standard belts suit light to medium-duty applications at CNC lathes, mills, and drill presses.</p>
<h3>Heavy-Duty Hinged Belt</h3>
<p>Heavy-duty hinged belts use larger pitches, thicker plates, hardened hinge pins, and reinforced side chains. Storch lists 4 in. pitch as heavy duty and 6 in. pitch as extra heavy duty. <a href="https://www.mayfran.com/products/material-and-scrap-handling-recycling/hot-forging-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayfran's hot forging conveyors</a> use 2.5, 4, 6, or 9 in. pitch hinged steel belts for parts that can reach 400 to 2,200°F. Heavy-duty belts handle large stamping scrap, die-cast runners, heavy turnings from large-diameter bar stock, and forging flash. They serve stamping press lines, large CNC turning centers, and foundry operations. Load ratings are set by the specific pitch, plate, and chain design, so get them in writing from the supplier.</p>
<h3>Perforated Hinged Belt</h3>
<p>Perforated hinged belts have holes punched in each plate to allow coolant drainage while still conveying chips. The perforations let cutting fluid pass through the belt into the collection trough below rather than being carried to the discharge point. Perforated belts are standard on wet machining applications where coolant recovery is important. Perforation size must be smaller than the smallest chip to prevent fine swarf from passing through.</p>
<h3>Magnetic Conveyors (the Main Alternative)</h3>
<p>Magnetic chip conveyors are a separate design, not a hinged belt with magnets. In a <a href="https://www.prab.com/beltless-magnetic-slide-conveyors-how-they-work-and-when-to-use-them-for-ferrous-chips-and-parts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">beltless magnetic slide conveyor</a>, permanent magnets on a chain run beneath a stationary stainless steel slider bed and drag ferrous chips along the top surface. The magnetic field holds fine chips on inclines without <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleats</a>. PRAB states these conveyors handle ferrous material only. Storch notes a steel hinge belt is often the better choice when material is non-ferrous, too heavy, too hot, or too bulky for a magnetic slider bed.</p>
