<p>Ratings vary by manufacturer and model. Confirm chip type, temperature, and incline limits with the supplier. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Hinged Steel Belt Applications</h2>
<p><strong>CNC machine chip removal:</strong> The primary application. Hinged steel belts sit beneath CNC lathes, machining centers, and grinders to collect and convey metal chips from the cutting zone to a chip bin. The belt handles all chip morphologies: continuous turnings from lathes, broken chips from mills, and fine swarf from grinders. Machine tool builders typically specify the chip conveyor opening and discharge height, so the conveyor is often sized to a specific machine model.</p>
<p><strong>Stamping press scrap:</strong> Hinged steel belts remove blanking slugs, trim scrap, and skeleton scrap from stamping presses. The belt sits in a pit beneath the press or at floor level behind the press, receiving scrap as it falls from the die. Heavy-duty belts handle the impact loading of large slugs dropping from progressive and transfer dies. Multiple presses feed a common trunk-line hinged belt conveyor that delivers all scrap to a central collection point.</p>
<p><strong>Laser and plasma cutting:</strong> Hinged belts collect cut parts, slugs, and dross from laser cutting tables and plasma cutters. The belt handles hot dross particles and sharp cut edges without damage. Some configurations position the belt below the cutting table's slat support system to catch falling parts and scrap simultaneously.</p>
<p><strong>Die casting and foundry:</strong> Hinged steel belts transport hot die-cast parts, runners, gates, and flash from die-cast machines and trim presses. The belt carries freshly cast aluminum and zinc parts while they are still hot. Coolant spray sections along the belt path cool parts during transport.</p>
<p><strong>Parts transfer:</strong> Beyond scrap, hinged steel belts transport finished parts between operations in metalworking plants. The rugged steel surface handles heavy, sharp, and oily parts that would damage rubber or plastic belts. Parts ride from one machine to the next for multi-operation processing.</p>
<p><strong>Coolant filtration systems:</strong> Hinged steel belts integrated with coolant tanks filter chips from cutting fluid. The belt lifts chips out of the coolant while fluid drains through the perforated belt back into the clean tank. This replaces standalone coolant filtration systems on machines with moderate chip volumes.</p>
<h2 id="sizing-heading">Sizing &amp; Selection</h2>
<h3>Belt Width</h3>
<p>Belt width must accommodate the chip discharge area of the machine tool. Available widths grow with pitch. Titan lists its <a href="https://www.titanconveyors.com/products/hinged-steel-belt/model-610-2-1-2-pitch-hinged-steel-belt-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model 610 2.5 in. pitch belt</a> in 6 to 24 in. widths. Jorgensen lists its <a href="https://www.jorgensenconveyors.com/product/hinged-steel-belt-conveyor-4-pitch/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">4 in. pitch hinged steel belt conveyor</a> in 6 to 84 in. widths. For stamping press scrap, belt width must exceed the die width to catch all falling scrap. For CNC machines, the machine manufacturer's chip conveyor opening dimension determines the required belt width.</p>
<h3>Belt Speed</h3>
<p>Hinged steel belts run slowly. Titan lists 5 to 30 FPM as typical for its Model 610, and Storch lists 30 FPM as standard for its 2.5 in. and larger pitches. Slower speeds suit continuous chip removal where a steady trickle of chips falls onto the belt. Faster speeds suit batch discharge from stamping presses where large volumes of scrap fall at once. Variable-speed drives allow the belt to run faster during heavy chip production and slower during light cuts.</p>
<h3>Discharge Height</h3>
<p>The discharge end of the belt inclines to dump material into a collection bin. Discharge height is set by the collection bin or hopper. Higher discharge requires a longer or steeper incline, which usually needs <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated</a> plates to hold material. Discharge height should clear the top of the collection bin with margin for material piling.</p>
<h3>Material Selection</h3>
<p>Carbon steel plates are standard and handle most metalworking applications. Stainless steel plates (304 or 316) serve applications with corrosive coolants, stainless steel machining, or food-adjacent manufacturing. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel conveyor guide</a> for alloy selection guidance. Hardened plates extend life in abrasive scrap applications but cost more.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Maintenance Guide</h2>
<p><strong>Daily:</strong> Follow lockout/tagout under <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.147" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147</a> before clearing jams. Check for chip jams at the discharge point and hinge pin area. Remove stringy turnings that wrap around the head sprocket. Verify coolant drainage is unobstructed. Inspect the belt for obvious plate damage or missing plates.</p>
<p><strong>Weekly:</strong> Inspect hinge pins for excessive wear. Check belt tracking and alignment. Verify the take-up mechanism maintains proper tension. Clean the coolant return trough of sludge and fine chip accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>Monthly:</strong> Measure chain elongation to predict replacement timing. Lubricate the drive gearbox per manufacturer specifications. Inspect sprocket teeth for wear patterns that indicate misalignment. Check motor amperage against baseline to detect increasing friction or overloading.</p>
<p><strong>Belt replacement:</strong> Individual damaged plates can be replaced by removing the hinge pins on either side, sliding out the damaged plate, and inserting a replacement. This field-serviceable design makes repairs faster than on <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">wire mesh belts</a>, which are usually repaired by splicing in a new section. Full belt replacement timing depends on chip type, load, temperature, and maintenance quality.</p>
